print("\n\nThe 'print()' fuction is pretty much the most basic and commonly used built-in function in the Python programming language, as it is used to make the computer write, or print something that you write in quotes inside the parentheses, like this: print('Hello, World!') This would print 'Hello, World!' to the screen in the terminal.\nYou can also print numbers, variables, and even the results of calculations.\nFor example, print(2 + 3) would print 5 to the screen.\nThe print function can also take multiple arguments separated by commas, which will be printed with a space in between.\nFor example, print('Hello', 'World') would print 'Hello World'.\nYou can also use the end parameter to change what is printed at the end of the output, for example, print('Hello', end='!') would print 'Hello!' without a newline at the end. A newline is basically just pressing enter, which is the default behavior of the print function.\nYou can also use the sep parameter to change what is printed between the arguments, for example, print('Hello', 'World', sep='-') would print 'Hello-World'.\n if you backslash, then n, it will print a newline character. I can't do it now because we are in a print function. The print function is very versatile and can be used in many different ways to display information to the user.\nIt is a fundamental part of programming in Python and is used in almost every program you will write.\n\nThank you for reading.\n\n")